# {{.Name}}

A product on **ultrastack** — one repo, one module, one binary. `main.go` is the
root page (`var App = …`, assigned exactly once); `internal/app/app.go` is the
only file that knows the feature list. `CONTEXT.md` is the domain glossary —
read it before naming anything.
{{if .From}}
Migrated from `{{.From}}` with `ultra new --from`: every operation is wired and
answers, but the handlers are **501 stubs** (`<feature>.<op>.not_implemented`)
until they are ported. Fill ONE stub at a time and keep the loop below green —
that is the whole migration plan. This product rides the TYPED api road (the
legacy edge, fully supported); forward products scaffold onto handler-standard
v3, and the laws below describe v3 where the two differ.
{{end}}
This file is a ROUTER, not the manual. It tells you where to look.

## The loop (non-negotiable, in this order)

1. `ultra vet ./...` — static wiring checks before anything runs. `-fix` applies
   the edits marked `[fixable]`; the rest need a decision. Blue `note[...]`
   lines are informational (the sibling-dependency listing) and never gate.
2. `ultra explain <code>` for a kernel/analyzer code (`DI0101`, `UV0009`), or
   `go run . explain <code>` for a preset code (`PG0101`, `WEB0202` — this
   binary knows the codes its own presets registered). **Never fix a diagnostic
   from memory:** the code carries the remedy, and the remedy changes.
3. `go doc github.com/bronystylecrazy/ultrastack/web Router` — read the
   signature. Never guess an API.
4. `go test ./...` after every change. `TestWiring` is the covenant: if
   `stack.Validate(App)` passes, `main` boots. `TestContractDrift` names any
   contract you changed — run `task contracts` and **commit the diff**; that
   diff is the change's blast radius, and hiding it is the bug.
5. **A gate named in a report was RE-RUN immediately before the report was
   written; paste its real output.** A claimed gate without a fresh run is the
   worst class of false report.

## The verbs

```
task dev          the whole inner loop in one terminal (= ultra dev): dev infra,
                  watch, rebuild, graceful restart, fresh contracts{{if .Web}}, web HMR{{end}}
task test         the covenant, the boot smoke, the drift gates
task contracts    regenerate the COMMITTED artifacts (openapi.json{{if .Web}} + the client{{end}})
{{- if .Web}}
task e2e          the golden path in a real browser against the built binary
{{- end}}
go run . <cmd>    the binary is the toolbox: routes, graph, doctor, codes,
                  config init, config reference, infra compose, openapi{{if .Web}}, client{{end}}
ultra new <name> --flags
                  scaffold another product. ALWAYS the flag form: bare
                  `ultra new` on a terminal opens an interactive wizard for a
                  human, and an agent must never be waiting on a prompt. The
                  wizard prints the equivalent flag command — use that
{{- if .MQTT}}
go run . topics   every bus subscription (the contract producers publish against)
go run . topics schemas    the declared payload types as JSON Schemas
go run . drops    the poison-message journal — code, topic, attempts, newest first
{{- end}}
{{- if .DB}}
ultra new table <name> "<cols>" [--owned]
                  the whole persistence slice: migration (created_at with NO
                  default), the five queries, a Store (store.go), then sqlc generate
cd internal/db && sqlc generate
                  after ANY edit to queries/*.sql — `ultra` is the sqlc plugin
                  that re-derives gen.DB, the error sentinels, the keyset page
                  wrapper and gen/factory. The SQL is yours; only gen/ is not
{{- end}}
```

A dev build serves **no frontend** by design — a 404 at `/` means you wanted
{{if .Web}}`task dev:web` or a `-tags embedspa` build{{else}}the embedspa build{{end}}, not a bug fix.

This product listens on **`:{{.HTTPPort}}`** — dev ports are derived from the
product name (config.toml's `[http] addr`{{if .Web}}, playwright's e2e port{{end}}), so
several products run side by side. They are ordinary files: change them freely.

## The handler laws (review-blockers)

- **Contracts are declared once, in Go.** `openapi.json`{{if .Web}} and
  `web/src/lib/api/`{{end}} are DERIVED and committed — never hand-edit them;
  fix the route chain and run `task contracts`.
- **A feature's HTTP surface is an `API` struct with a fluent route table** —
  `func (a *API) Handle(router web.Router)`, wired as `web.Provide(NewAPI)` in
  the feature's `Module` (compile-checked spelling: `web.Register[*API]()`).
  The chain IS the contract: `.Produce[T]()` / `.Accept[T]()` /
  `.Fails(status, Code)` / `.Require(Scope)` / `.Public()` / `.Internal()` /
  `.Undocumented(reason)` — OpenAPI, the TS client and `./app routes` are all
  derived from it.
- **Handlers are raw fiber, and the binding is EXPLICIT.** `c.Bind()`,
  `strconv` on params, your own status decisions; failures ride the shared
  envelope through `web.Fail(c, status, Code, format, ...)`. No magic.
- **Surface naming is a closed vocabulary.** `API` is the HTTP surface,
  `Events` is the MQTT surface (`Handle(m mqtt.Router)`, wired with
  `mqtt.Provide(NewEvents)`) — one transport per struct, and Go enforces it
  (both interfaces name their method `Handle`). `Live` (realtime) and `Jobs`
  (background work) are RESERVED for the same pattern; do not spend those
  names on anything else.
- **Scopes are constants, never literals** (UV0011). `const ReadScope =
  web.Scope("{{.Name}}.read")` on the contract page; `[auth.roles]` grants
  them. An inline string in `.Require` is a lint finding.
- **Middleware laws.** `.Require` REPLACES the group default — the two never
  stack (`.Public()` replaces in both directions). The chain runs `Use` →
  group → inline → handler, in declaration order — no phases, no priorities.
  Enforcement runs BEFORE any feature middleware: an unauthenticated request
  on a guarded route never reaches your code. Auth happens ONLY via
  `.Require`/`.Public()` — never write an auth-checking middleware.
- **Declaration order is match order.** Fiber matches routes in the order
  declared: static paths before parameterized siblings (`/ping` before
  `/:id`), or `/:id` swallows them.
- **Every route declares `.Require(Scope)` or `.Public()`.** Secure by
  default: forgetting is boot error WEB0202, not a silent hole.
- **Pointer responses:** return `*T` bodies (`c.JSON(&t)`), and a nil pointer
  only ever travels beside a non-nil error.
- **Single-purpose returns.** After a branch on `err`, never `return v, err` —
  one return serves one purpose: `(v, nil)` or `(zero, err)`. Pass-through
  delegation (`return a.store.Get(...)`) is the legal exception.
- **`diag.Wrap` with named facts, never `fmt.Errorf("%w")`.** Wrap at the
  point that KNOWS the meaning of the values: `diag.Wrap(err, "creating
  {{.Name}}", "id", id)`. Evidence rides the failure log, never the wire.
- **Store-boundary error translation.** Driver sentinels (`pgx.ErrNoRows`,
  `gen.Err...Taken`) die in store.go: the store translates them to the
  feature's own sentinels (`ErrNotFound`), and handlers branch on those.
- **The feature ladder.** Rung 1: the whole feature in `<feature>.go` (what
  `ultra new feature` writes). Rung 2: split by responsibility into the closed
  grammar — `<feature>.go` (front page + Module), `api.go` (the HTTP surface),
  `events.go` (the bus surface), `store.go` (persistence), `errors.go` (codes
  + sentinels + scopes) — plus plain-noun files. Rung 3: past ~8 files or a
  second route prefix (UV0009 nudges), grow a CHILD package one level deep,
  wired by the parent's own `di.Module` — never a line in app.go.
- **The import law.** Features MAY import sibling features, ONE direction —
  `ultra vet` lists the surface (note[UV0010]). A would-be cycle cannot
  compile, and wanting one is a design signal with three exits: move the
  shared logic down (`internal/kind` — values only, stdlib-only imports),
  publish an event, or merge the features. `di.Alias` seams in app.go remain
  available when you want compile-time decoupling.
- **Error codes in `errors.go` are contracts.** Clients localize from them and
  they ride OpenAPI's `x-error-codes` (declare with `.Fails`). Never rename a
  shipped code — add one.
{{- if .MQTT}}
- **Bus handlers make one of three terminal decisions**: `nil` (ack),
  `mqtt.Drop(c, Code, ...)` (poison — never retried, journaled, `./app
  drops`), any other error (redelivered per the entry's `.Retry`). Bind
  explicitly (`c.Bind(&ev)`); declare payloads with `.Accept[T]()`. Handlers
  are idempotent (at-least-once delivery); durable work goes through
  `jobs.Enqueue`, not a retry loop. A feature module using `di.Export` must
  also export `mqtt.Handler`.
{{- end}}
{{- if .Web}}
- **Frontend tooling is bun, exclusively.** `bun install`, `bun add`, `bunx`
  (never npx), `bun run` — no npm, node, yarn or pnpm anywhere, including
  scripts you generate.
- **The generated client IS the API surface.** Screens import the typed fetch
  functions from `$lib/api`; a hand-rolled `fetch('/api/…')` fails
  `bun run check` and needs an entry in `web/api-usage-allow.txt` naming why.
  (`$lib/auth.svelte.ts` is the one scaffolded escape — the session store over
  the framework's /auth routes.)
{{- if .Auth}}
- **The login page and the redirect guard are separable.** `+layout.ts` holds
  the whole guard — imports included — between its `auth guard` and
  `end auth guard` markers. A PUBLIC app (docs, marketing) that still wires
  `auth.Use()` deletes marker-to-marker and keeps `login/+page.svelte`. That
  deletion is SANCTIONED, not a scaffold violation; everything else in that
  file stays.
{{- end}}
- **Form controls never appear bare in a form** — they compose under `Field`
  (label, description, errors, a11y wiring). Bare controls are for toolbars and
  search boxes. The UI doctrine — the design system, its traps, and `Field`
  composition — is `references/ui.md` in the vendored skill below.
- **A screen built from a Figma frame resolves every instance through the design
  system's `code-connect.map.json`** — never hand-rolled markup for a component
  that exists, and a `TODO(figma)` naming any node that does not resolve. The
  workflow is `references/figma-product.md`.
{{- end}}
- **A bug fix lands with the test that would have caught it**; a feature lands
  with one test of its central claim. Raw fiber means no signature proves a
  response type — the feature's contract test does, with `webtest` (see the
  taught block in `contract_test.go`).
- **No unnecessary helper functions.** Extract only at the second call site or
  for a genuinely separate concern; once-used logic stays inline. Nothing
  unused lands.
- **Comments are concise and short.** One line stating a constraint the code
  cannot show; never narrate the next line or restate the obvious.
- **Time comes from `di.Clock`.** Stamp rows in Go (`clock.Now()`); no
  `DEFAULT now()` in migrations — tests must be able to drive time.

