## Requirements & traceability (this product is opted in)

`requirements/` is present, so the phase-2 compliance laws bind here — the
full model is `references/trace.md` in the vendored skill:

- **Draft the REQ before the feature.** `ultra new requirement <id> "<title>"`
  first; the code lands against an existing (ideally approved) requirement.
- **Joins land in the SAME commit as the work.** A REQ's `operations[]` /
  `tests[]` / `e2e[]` frontmatter moves with the code it claims. No
  requirement ids in code, ever — the operationId is the join key.
- **Ask, don't guess.** Unsure about intent or acceptance? `ultra req ask
  <id> "<question>"` parks it as `- open:` and `ultra trace` renders the
  requirement blocked-on-human. Batch questions; answers are written back
  into the file as dated Q&As — asked once, answered forever.
- **Stop for approval.** Only a HUMAN runs `ultra req approve` (it stamps
  who/when — the validation record). Never edit `status:`/`approved:` by
  hand, and never supply an approver's identity on someone's behalf.
- **Statuses are derived.** `ultra trace` computes implemented / verified /
  validated from openapi.json and the recorded runs (`task test` / `task
  e2e` tee `.ultra/test.json` / `.ultra/e2e.json`). Run `ultra trace` before
  reporting progress; a stale record renders unknown, never green.
{{- end}}

## The full doctrine

**Run `ultra skill install` first, before anything else.** It vendors
`.claude/skills/ultrastack/` — the framework's own SKILL.md and `references/`,
at the EXACT version this product's `go.mod` pins. A fresh `ultra new` scaffold
arrives WITHOUT that directory — always — so this is a required first step, not
a fallback for when something is missing. Once it is there,
`ultra skill install --check` reports any drift from the pin. The handler
standard's own pages are `references/presets/web.md` (HTTP) and
`references/presets/events.md` (MQTT).

`.mcp.json` at the product root wires the same doctrine as tools: every Claude
Code session started in this directory picks it up (approve it once, the first
time you run `claude` here).

`ultra contrib list` prints this product's capabilities: the presets it wires
(with the spelling it used) and the ones it has not.
